# Feature 0.0.7: discovery-led ZFS-backed Samba ingest

Release 0.0.7 is the complete LXC/Samba feature boundary. It composes the
existing Debian LXC, host networking, ZFS, container networking, and Samba
features around an already accepted host dataset. The release includes
discovery-led untagged or VLAN-backed data networking, mapped dataset
permissions, a hardened two-share Samba role, static-destination egress, and
the later proxy-backed egress extension. Whole-GPU passthrough remains 0.0.8
and Local Model Inventory remains 0.0.9.

The feature must remain independent of hostname, IP address, interface name,
bridge name, VLAN, CTID, pool, dataset, mountpoint, account, and subnet.
Acceptance fixtures use synthetic values that are never repository defaults.

## DATA-Link for SMB Share Local LAN

The physical-link and host-bridge work is the DATA-Link for SMB Share Local
LAN capability. It is speed-neutral above the 1Gbps safety floor: discovery
ranks faster interfaces when present, but neither the feature name nor its
configuration assumes a particular Ethernet speed.

## Required traffic separation

- Proxmox web administration and host SSH stay on a discovered management
  path and are accepted only from operator-selected management CIDRs.
- A container egress interface owns the only default route. Its bridge,
  address method, interface name, and Proxmox net slot are discovered live.
- A separate data interface uses an operator-selected bridge and static local
  address with no gateway.
- The data bridge may be untagged or VLAN-aware. Untagged mode writes no VLAN
  attributes and gives the Proxmox host no data-network address.
- Samba binds only to the selected data-role interface. TCP 445 is accepted
  only from selected local data CIDRs.
- The Proxmox firewall protects the host and virtual NIC boundary. UFW inside
  the container is the container firewall authority.
- Container SSH and SFTP are disabled for the accepted ingest role. Operators
  use the Proxmox console or `pct exec`.

## Gate 1: discovery and immutable network intent

Preflight is read-only and must inventory:

- default routes, bridges, bonds, VLANs, host addresses, and cluster links;
- every physical NIC's current name, aliases, MAC, PCI identity, driver,
  supported/current speed, carrier, state, routes, addresses, and master;
- every LXC/VM NIC slot, configured MAC, bridge, address mode, tag/trunks,
  firewall flag, and runtime guest interface/route state;
- existing Samba binding and firewall state when discoverable.

Management is derived from the active default route and the physical members
behind it. A data candidate must be physical, outside management/cluster/
storage roles, unaddressed, unrouted, and unused by another bridge or bond.
Candidates may be ranked by driver, PCI label, and supported speed, but the
operator must select one. The data path requires a separate physical NIC with
at least 1Gbps of current, advertised, or known driver capability; unknown or
sub-gigabit candidates fail closed. The reviewed selection stores MAC and PCI
identity; the interface name is resolved again immediately before apply.

Synthetic fixtures contain a management bridge, multiple unused physical
candidates, and virtual `veth`/`fw*` devices. Tests must exclude virtual
devices, derive management from the default route, and refuse selection based
on a familiar fixture name or MAC.

Network execution is split into explicit stages:

1. `preflight`: read-only inventory and risk report.
2. `probe`: temporarily bring one unused candidate up, wait for carrier,
   inspect it with ethtool, then restore its prior state unless applying.
3. `write`: save a checksummed backup, stage the reviewed bridge block, and
   require both `ifreload -a -s -n` parser validation and `ifreload -a -n`
   reload-plan validation to pass without structural warnings.
4. `apply`: revalidate identity and management invariants, reload networking,
   and activate the selected data link.
5. `verify`: recollect facts and compare them to immutable intent.
6. `rollback`: restore the exact prior interfaces file from local console.

Untagged bridge mode renders a manual physical port and a manual Linux bridge
with no host IP, gateway, VLAN awareness, or bridge VID list. The bridge name
is discovered and confirmed from currently unused bridge names.

Apply fails if management identity or route changes, selected hardware drifts,
the target bridge conflicts, a selected NIC becomes used/addressed, candidate
parsing emits structural warnings, reload fails, or physical carrier is absent.
Warnings from a complete feature-owned legacy block may be accepted only as
repair input when they name the selected NIC or bridge. The canonical staged
candidate must eliminate duplicate-interface, invalid bridge-attribute, and
unrecognized-interface warnings before write or apply can succeed.

## Gate 2: role-based LXC networking

The feature must not assume network slots, guest interface names, DHCP,
container IDs, or bridge names. It selects a container from inventory, maps PVE NIC slots to
runtime guest names by MAC, and identifies egress from the live default route.

The operator confirms the existing egress role. The tool discovers a free PVE
slot and collision-free guest interface name for the data role. It may suggest
a data address by combining the selected subnet with the Proxmox management
address's final octet, but it must require confirmation and duplicate-address
probing. The serialized data NIC has a static address, firewall enabled, no
gateway, and no tag in untagged mode.

After `pct set`, the tool first verifies hot-apply. It requests a controlled
restart only when pending/runtime evidence proves one is required. Acceptance
requires exactly one default route, egress traffic on the selected egress
role, and local data traffic on the selected data role.

## Gate 3: dataset attachment and mapped identity

1. Verify the accepted ZFS pool, dataset, properties, health, and mountpoint.
2. Select the host mount and independent container mount from live inventory.
3. Create a locked, non-login service identity inside the unprivileged LXC.
4. Resolve the identity's effective host UID/GID from the actual LXC mapping.
5. Preserve existing `root:root` ownership. Apply service ACLs to existing
   content in restartable batches and default ACLs to new content.
6. Never use recursive ownership replacement, mode 0777, host-root mapping,
   or Samba root-forcing as a fallback.
7. Verify read, create, rename, delete, POSIX ACL, and xattr behavior as the
   mapped identity before starting Samba.
8. Reboot the container and host to prove mount and mapping persistence.

## Gate 4: Samba policy

One selected dataset is exported through two shares:

- guest read-only, forced to the mapped service identity;
- authenticated read/write, restricted to the selected Samba account.

The accepted policy uses SMB3, file mode 0660, directory mode 0770, and the
`catia`, `fruit`, and `streams_xattr` VFS stack. It disables SMB1, printers,
Avahi, WSDD, broad private-network allowances, world-write modes, and root
forcing. `interfaces`, `hosts allow`, and firewall rules are derived from the
reviewed data role and CIDRs.

The default compatibility policy derives the authenticated username and
initial password from the discovered LXC hostname, with a prominent warning.
The runner also offers an explicit custom username and hidden, confirmed
password path. Secrets are never committed, printed in public evidence, or
retained in persistent facts or temporary variables files after execution.

## Gate 5: container hardening and egress

The ingest role installs only required runtime packages. It stops, disables,
and masks SSH services and sockets; locks temporary login accounts; disables
unneeded discovery and client tooling; and keeps nesting/FUSE disabled.

UFW defaults to deny incoming and outgoing. It permits DHCP and selected DNS
through the egress role, SMB responses and TCP 445 on the data role, and only
reviewed outbound destination IP/CIDR and port tuples. IPv6 remains disabled
until an equivalent policy is supplied.

Approved URL metadata is validated by a download helper, but static firewall
enforcement remains IP/port based and redirects are refused. The final URL
must be explicitly approved. The proxy-backed 0.0.7 extension will later
provide hostname policy, exact-URL broker downloads, checksums, size limits,
approval windows, and audit logs without changing the network role model.

## Public interfaces and publication

All runners consume one schema-versioned, operator-reviewed YAML policy whose
site values begin unset. Discovery writes facts separately from intent. Plans
include source snapshot hashes and cannot apply after hardware, management,
container, or configuration drift.

Every new runner, helper, playbook, schema, and support file is added to the
Pages manifest and local/published dependency validation in the same change.
Intermediate feature commits may publish to Pages for remote acceptance, but
no official 0.0.7 tag is created until every gate, including proxy egress,
passes review.

## Contract tests

Automated coverage includes:

- arbitrary physical/virtual interface names and synthetic link fixtures;
- down data-link candidates, explicit activation, carrier errors, and MAC/PCI drift;
- management bridges, bonds, direct interfaces, and ambiguous default routes;
- bridge conflicts, failed reload dry runs, and carrier failures;
- arbitrary LXC net slots, guest names, DHCP/static egress, and one-route
  enforcement;
- untagged data NIC serialization with no tag or gateway;
- duplicate-address rejection and hot-apply versus restart detection;
- unprivileged UID/GID translation and resumable ACL application;
- guest-read/authenticated-write rendering, SMB3, xattrs, and no root force;
- stopped/masked SSH and default-deny static egress;
- idempotent reruns, rollback artifacts, reboot persistence, and complete
  Pages publication.

## Parser source-of-truth rule and incident history

DATA-Link preflight exposed a layered-escaping failure while reading an
existing `iface` stanza: production captured `manual` as `ma`. Doubled escape
sequences in a negated regex character class were interpreted as excluded
literal letters. The CI fixture used a separately copied parser with different
escaping, so it tested intended behavior rather than the shipped parser.

The feature therefore treats
`ansible/proxmox/tasks/data-link.candidate.yml` as the canonical parser,
policy, renderer, and structural validator. The live VLAN playbook and test
fixtures include that exact task. Future feature parsers follow the repository
[feature-authoring guide](../../development/feature-authoring.md): prefer
field parsing, execute production artifacts from tests, cover LF/CRLF and
whitespace variants, compare complete tokens, and reject duplicated fixture
parsers through policy checks.

## Human acceptance sequence

1. Accept host/runtime compatibility and read-only network inventory.
2. Select and probe the physical data NIC; do not infer it from its name.
3. Review, write, apply, and verify the host data bridge while management stays
   reachable through an out-of-band console.
4. Discover and confirm the LXC egress role, then add the no-gateway data role.
5. Accept mapped storage permissions and xattrs without changing ownership.
6. Apply the two-share Samba role and container firewall/hardening policy.
7. From a data-network client, prove guest read denial of writes,
   authenticated create/rename/delete/xattr, reconnect, and sustained transfer.
8. Prove host SSH/web access is management-CIDR-only, LXC SSH is unavailable,
   unapproved egress fails, and the data bridge has no host address.
9. Reboot the container and host, then repeat route, mount, ACL, SMB, firewall,
   and ZFS health checks.
10. Preserve private plans, checksums, facts, rollback files, and acceptance
    evidence for release review.
